window.ENG = {"headline":"Start with the animals backend. It now sends the status SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED to PIMS on events whose published schemas allow only SUBMITTED, and that is the one high-severity finding. Next, in the same area, look at the cancel-amend status that can disagree with the documents held. Then look at the plants confirmation date, which is wrong by a day for the first hour after midnight in summer because the backend saves a time with no time zone. The tests were widened to accept the wrong day, so they hide it. Third, the workspace build gate, which can take down the stack and delete the lease that the run itself holds. In the frontends, begin with the backend fetch timeout and retry change. It does nothing useful on CDP, splits backend calls across two fetch paths, and only the local test paths exercise it. The same area also covers the raised FIT timeout. Then check that subdivision codes picked as transit countries reach GBN-AG as raw country codes, that the INS frontend still drops fetch causes from its logs, and that the plants Check your answers links send a trader round in a loop. The remaining findings are low severity: test strength, stub provenance, build-loop edge cases, comments and accessibility.","reviewed":11,"refuted":7,"order":[{"rank":1,"area":"Animals backend","priority":1,"why":"It holds the only high-severity finding, a status value that breaks the published GBN-AG contract to PIMS, plus a stale-status defect on cancel-amend."},{"rank":2,"area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"why":"Its date checks were widened to hide a live one-day-off product bug, and several other assertions are looser than they should be."},{"rank":3,"area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"why":"The build gate can delete the run's own stack lease after Docker Desktop restarts, which affects lease safety and run continuity."},{"rank":4,"area":"Performance tests","priority":1,"why":"The k6 walk drifts slightly from the real opening run and logs a failure line, including the sign-in callback URL, on every iteration."},{"rank":5,"area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"why":"The code is sound; what remains is fixture provenance, overclaimed stub fidelity and test hygiene."},{"rank":6,"area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"why":"It must head the frontend list: the change does nothing on CDP, forks the fetch path, is tested through a library mock, and sits beside a raised FIT timeout."},{"rank":7,"area":"Animals frontend shared code","priority":2,"why":"Subdivision codes now chosen as transit and destination countries reach GBN-AG unmapped, and amend Save and return behaves differently from page to page."},{"rank":8,"area":"INS frontend","priority":2,"why":"The new cause logging misses the countries 503 and every error caught inside a controller, so the diagnostic gap it set out to close is still open."},{"rank":9,"area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"why":"It repeats the subdivision-to-GBN-AG risk and adds smaller contradictions in titles, accessibility and comments."},{"rank":10,"area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"why":"Check your answers error links can trap a trader in a loop, and a Welsh date, a cross-feature import and comment hygiene are open."}],"areas":[{"key":"backend","title":"Animals backend","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed all of origin/main...HEAD in trade-imports-animals-backend (PR #107, 4 commits). I also followed the new status downstream into the outbox, trade-imports-dynamics-gateway, trade-imports-ins-backend and the GBN-AG schemas in trade-imports-schemas. Inside the service the change holds up: status guards, null handling on cancel-amend for older records, the projection fields and the unit coverage are all sound. The serious problem is the wire contract. Every submit without a scanned ITAHC now sends notificationStatusCode SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED to PIMS on events whose declared schemas pin it to the constant SUBMITTED, and trade-imports-schemas was not changed. A second, medium defect: cancel-amend restores a stored status that can disagree with the documents actually held.","survived":3,"killed":0},{"key":"stub-refdata","title":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed both changes against origin/main: the stub (inc-173: controllers now serve captured MDM JSON as-is, the six DTOs are deleted, new fixtures and tests) and reference-data (inc-007: PortType, PortListOrder, the traffic mapping and its tests). I checked the fixtures with jq against the raw CDP-test captures and against every consumer. The code is sound. The fixtures match the captures: 250 countries, 32 in the GBNAG_SPS_EX block, 78 ports whose traffic is all Airport, Port or Rail, as in the raw capture. reference-data's model still reads the new shape, and nothing else reads the stub's old fields. What remains is one fixture-provenance gap and three smaller risks, none of them a runtime defect today.","survived":4,"killed":0},{"key":"tests-repo","title":"Tests repo","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed every change in trade-imports-ins-tests from origin/main...HEAD (124 files, 61 commits), comparing the assertions in each changed spec against main, plus the page objects, flows, seeding helpers and commit 65b6afc. Most spec changes follow the Design Release 2.1 rulings, and many assertions are stricter than before: exact toHaveText where main used toContainText, full innerText address checks, and new states for Amend, Submitted and Submitted action required. None of the changes made to beat a flake (the test.slow() calls, the seeded pagination spec, the reordered beforeEach in the read-only address-book specs) dropped or weakened an assertion. The real risks are elsewhere: date checks that now accept the wrong calendar day, an INS dashboard test that locks in a raw status enum shown to users, one assertion that can never fail, a page-object heading that now matches several pages, and reference-list predicates left loose after the themes were merged. Commit 65b6afc is superseded and not needed.","survived":4,"killed":2},{"key":"perf","title":"Performance tests","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed the whole trade-imports-performance-tests diff (origin/main...HEAD, 3 commits, 7 files) against the live-animals frontend's new RUN_STEPS and flow.js on the combined branch. The import-reason move is correct: the first six k6 steps now match Design Release 2.1. After additional-details, though, the k6 walk still does not follow the real opening run, and the change missed the frontend dropping cph-number from the run. Neither shows up in any check, because walker.reach quietly opens a page directly when the previous save landed somewhere else. No threshold or measurement was weakened. The new missed-page logging is useful but only covers the INS dashboard, and it logs on every iteration, including the OIDC callback URL.","survived":2,"killed":0},{"key":"workspace-tooling","title":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"summary":"I reviewed the workspace tooling diff (3c28ff5a~1..HEAD) across tim exec (stack, stack-lease, stack-refresh, stack-fingerprints, docker-daemon), tim build gate, tim backlog split, split-off and themes, tim distil, and the increment-build-loop workflow. The backlog split-off, relink, distil and x-refresh-with code is sound and well tested. The main defect: when Docker Desktop has quit, tim now starts it again inside every `docker ps`, and the gate then treats the run's own down stack as one it started itself. It releases that stack after the e2e rungs, which takes it down and deletes the run's lease. So the new recover-and-re-lease path almost never runs. A second risk: the new rollback step in the failed-attempt handler soft-resets every unpushed commit in each repo, not just the failed attempt's. Neither lets wrong code land green; both affect lease safety and keeping the branch's history intact.","survived":5,"killed":0},{"key":"backend-fetch","title":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"summary":"I reviewed backend-fetch.js and its tests, the vitest set-up shim and the errors.js cause logging. The animals and plants frontends carry the same file (blob d1664a3 in both). I also reviewed the animals FIT timeout raise and checked both against the build's stack logs and the GitHub Actions runs. On CDP the change does nothing useful, because HTTP_PROXY is set there, so the 2s bound never applies and the retry almost never fires. It also splits backend calls into two fetch/FormData paths, and no test environment now runs the CDP one. It was tested through a library mock that hides what it sends. My verdict is to remove it from production code, keeping stackWithCauses. The FIT timeout raise covers real set-up cost, not flakes (0 retries).","survived":5,"killed":0},{"key":"animals-shared","title":"Animals frontend shared code","priority":2,"summary":"I reviewed every file the build changed in trade-imports-animals-frontend outside the feature folders: shared kit, save-actions, amend-form-actions, the lib/validate soft save, the navigation change from nextInSection to nextInTaskRow, run.js steps and Back targets, the entry guard, task rows and flow sections, the new SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED status in records, the real and stub marshal/write guards and lifecycle, the countries and ports services, errors.js, context.js, the FIT changes and the test setup. backend-fetch.js was skipped as asked. Most of it is sound. Submit is still gated on readyForCheckYourAnswers, so a soft save cannot submit invalid data. isSubmittedStatus is used everywhere a status is checked, write guards still block submitted records, and the reordered sections leave the ENFORCED_AT_CONTINUE prerequisites unchanged. Only live-animals is under sets/, so there is no germinal set for the nextTarget change to alter, and I found no navigation loops or dead ends. The one finding worth reviewing hard is the new subdivision codes reaching GBN-AG unmapped.","survived":2,"killed":1},{"key":"plants-shared","title":"Plants frontend shared code","priority":2,"summary":"I reviewed every plants-frontend change outside the per-page feature folders, on feat/NO_JIRA-design-release-2-1-combined against origin/main. That covered validate allowMissing, nextInTaskRow, the opening run now ending on check your answers, the entry guard's not-found pass-through, amendFormActions with the amend save-actions macro, the journey strip's Cancel amend link, page titles, port option text, stackWithCauses and the vitest undici setup file. I compared each against the animals copy. This shared code is largely sound: run.js, navigation.js, amend-form-actions.js, context.js and save-actions.njk match animals except for the plants-only besidePrimary, and the targeted unit suites pass (19 files, 394 tests). I found no correctness defect. There are two low findings: an unbounded loop over error causes in the new error logging, and one gap in soft save between the two frontends that someone needs to confirm was intended.","survived":0,"killed":2},{"key":"ins-frontend","title":"INS frontend","priority":2,"summary":"I reviewed the whole trade-imports-ins-frontend diff from origin/main to HEAD (7 commits, 26 files). The page-title change follows the GOV.UK pattern in both English and Welsh, and its tests are sound. The SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED link matches inc-113 and the animals backend's status enum. The countries stub now loads from a captured fixture, and GB is still added by address-countries.js. The capture script is a harmless dev script. The real problem is errors.js. Its cause-chain logging logs no secrets or personal data, but it misses most of the failures it was written to explain. Two weaker points remain: a cause chain with no end, and a raw status enum shown to users.","survived":3,"killed":2},{"key":"animals-features","title":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"summary":"I sampled the live-animals feature folders in trade-imports-animals-frontend (diff against origin/main). The sample covered check-answers (controller, template, view-header, species-errors, incomplete-cards, documents, commodity cards), import-reason, origin, transit-countries, commodities/search, confirmation and cancel-amend, plus their unit and fit tests. The area is mostly clean. Nothing in the diff touches the obligation model. No new vi.mock or spy calls appear in the feature tests. The fit specs walk the journey rather than seed it, and seeding happens only in the in-memory unit tests. No assertion was weakened to beat a flake; a few long walks had their timeout raised to 90s. Of the seven findings, the two that matter most are a cross-repo risk (subdivision codes now saved as transit countries go to GBN-AG as country codes) and an inconsistency between pages in whether 'Save and return' during an amendment skips the required-answer checks. The rest are low: the draft review's page title contradicts an earlier acceptance criterion, a maxlength attribute conflicts with the prefix-forgiving parser, a new cross-feature import of a controller, region landmarks on the hub, and explanatory comments added to source.","survived":7,"killed":0},{"key":"plants-features","title":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"summary":"I reviewed the diff against origin/main for the sampled plants feature folders: check-answers (controller, view-model, outstanding-rows, summary rows, template, copy, tests), arrival-details, origin, commodity-type, declaration and cancel-amend. I also read the shared helpers they now depend on: validate allowMissing, kit.nextTarget/exitTarget, save-actions and amend-form-actions. One real behaviour defect is worth close review. Check your answers now lists one error per unfinished task row, but each error links to the row's first page with ?change=1, and saving there goes straight back to Check your answers. So a row that is half done traps the trader in a loop, and a unit test fixes that link in place. The other findings are low. Tests drive the UI rather than seeding journeys, the only mocks are the address-book and records boundaries, the markup stays within GOV.UK Frontend components, and the new HTML address rows escape their input.","survived":4,"killed":0}],"findings":[{"rank":1,"ranks":[1],"id":"animals-backend-f1","area":"Animals backend","priority":1,"title":"SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED sent to PIMS on events whose schemas require SUBMITTED","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/notification/NotificationService.java","line":210,"what":"submitNotification and cancelAmendNotification now write the aggregate with status SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED. OutboxService then serialises it unchanged into two places: data.exchangedDocument.notificationStatusCode (through ExchangedDocument.from, status.name()) and statusChanges[].status. The events are NotificationSubmitted, NotificationSubmissionAmended and NotificationAmendmentCancelled, and each carries a schemaUrl. The schemas those URLs name all pin the value with {\"const\": \"SUBMITTED\"}: trade-imports-schemas gbn-ag-event-notification-submitted-v1, -submission-amended-v1 and -amendment-cancelled-v1. The GBN-AG status codelist (codelists/gbn-ag-notification-status.json) and gbn-ag-state-transitions.md know only DRAFT, SUBMITTED, AMEND and DELETED. trade-imports-schemas is still on main and has no change for the new status. The dynamics gateway passes the string straight through to PIMS (PimsGbnAgDataMapper/V2 pass doc.notificationStatusCode() through; OutboxStatusChange.status is a String). Sam's add-only ruling (claim 42) mapped the new status in the animals frontend and the INS frontend, but not in the external GBN-AG/PIMS contract. NotificationIT line 938 now pins the non-conforming value as expected behaviour, and four older outbox ITs were changed from SUBMITTED to SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED.","scenario":"A trader submits a live-animals notification without a scanned ITAHC. The ITs show this is now the default for any notification with no document. The outbox writes a NotificationSubmitted event that declares schemaUrl gbn-ag-event-notification-submitted-v1.schema.json but carries notificationStatusCode \"SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED\". The gateway forwards it to PIMS over Azure Service Bus. PIMS either fails schema validation, or does not recognise the code and does not treat the notification as submitted. The BCP and caseworkers then never see a pre-notification the trader believes was submitted. Each later amend, cancel-amend or resubmit repeats the problem.","severity":"high","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Do not ship until the wire contract is settled. Option 1: keep the GBN-AG wire status as SUBMITTED by mapping SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED to SUBMITTED in ExchangedDocument.from and when building StatusChange. Carry the outstanding items as their own field if downstream needs them; this also means the INS backend (NotificationUpsertService reads status only from notificationStatusCode) needs another way to show 'action required'. Option 2: add the code to the gbn-ag codelist and minted v2 event schemas in trade-imports-schemas, agreed with the PIMS team, on the same branch name. Either way, add an IT asserting a clean-ITAHC submit's event carries notificationStatusCode SUBMITTED.","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch. NotificationStatus gains SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED (diff). submitNotification (NotificationService.java:202-212) uses it whenever OutstandingItem.outstandingFor finds no COMPLETE ITAHC or HEALTH_CERTIFICATE. That covers no upload and also an ITAHC whose scan is still PENDING at submit. cancelAmendNotification (line 288) can restore it too. ExchangedDocument.from (outbox/gbnag/ExchangedDocument.java:32) writes status.name() into notificationStatusCode, and OutboxService:74-76 appends it to statusChanges. OutboxEventType points the schemaUrl at gbn-ag-event-notification-submitted-v1, -submission-amended-v1 and -amendment-cancelled-v1. In trade-imports-schemas all three pin exchangedDocument.notificationStatusCode to {\"const\": \"SUBMITTED\"} (checked submitted-v1 line 26), and that repo has no commits on the branch. The dynamics gateway's PimsGbnAgDataMapper and PimsGbnAgDataMapperV2 pass the string through unchanged. Sam's ruling claim 42 lists only the animals frontend, the INS frontend and the stub. His earlier ruling (point 8, 2026-10-09) says no PIMS profile or schema change is made in this programme. So the new value contradicts the declared contract and the stated scope. NotificationIT:938 pins the non-conforming value as correct. Whether PIMS actually rejects the event is unproven, but sending it breaches the declared contract, so high stands.","also":[]},{"rank":2,"ranks":[2],"id":"animals-backend-f2","area":"Animals backend","priority":1,"title":"Cancel-amend restores a stale outstanding-items status while document changes made during the amendment stay","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/notification/NotificationService.java","line":288,"what":"cancelAmendNotification sets the status from the outstandingItems kept from the last submission. Accompanying documents live in their own collection: an upload made during the amendment is not rolled back on cancel, and a deletion (DocumentService.deleteByUploadId, a hard delete) is not restored. Only notification content and fulfilments are restored. The restored status can therefore contradict the documents the notification holds, and the documents the AmendmentCancelled event lists in referenceDocument (OutboxService reads the live COMPLETE documents). The designed path for clearing 'action required' is Continue, which starts an amendment (inc-113, c-144), so traders will take this path.","scenario":"Case A. A notification is SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED. The trader clicks Continue (amend), uploads an ITAHC that scans COMPLETE, then clicks Cancel amend. The status reverts to SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED and the dashboard warns that the certificate is missing, while the event's referenceDocument lists the clean ITAHC. Case B. A notification is SUBMITTED with an ITAHC. The trader amends, deletes the ITAHC, then cancels. The status becomes SUBMITTED with outstandingItems [], but no health certificate exists, and PIMS receives SUBMITTED with no ITAHC reference.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Recompute on cancel as submit does: OutstandingItem.outstandingFor(documentService.findByNotificationRef(referenceNumber)), then set both outstandingItems and the status from that result. Alternatively, snapshot the accompanying-document set at amend and restore it on cancel. Add an IT for the amend, upload ITAHC, cancel-amend path.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. cancelAmendNotification (line 288) takes the status from the outstandingItems stored at the last submit and never re-reads the documents. Nothing under accompanyingdocument/ mentions amend or cancel, and uploads and deletes have no notification-status guard. So documents added or removed during an amendment survive a cancel, while the status reverts. Meanwhile OutboxService:91 lists the live COMPLETE documents in the event. Case A is the realistic one: the designed recovery is amend, upload the ITAHC, then submit, and a trader who uploads and then cancels stays at action-required with a clean ITAHC on file. The documents not rolling back is older behaviour. The stale status that contradicts them is new in this diff. Medium is right.","also":[]},{"rank":3,"ranks":[3],"id":"tests-repo-f1","area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"title":"Date checks accept yesterday at any hour, hiding an off-by-one-day bug","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/plants/e2e/features/review.spec.ts","line":201,"what":"Commit 3e7b227 widened the plants confirmation 'Date of notification' check to accept yesterday, today or tomorrow. The animals view's 'Date created' and 'Date submitted' checks (notification-view-states.spec.ts:40, 183, 266) and the plants ones (review.spec.ts:412, 629) accept yesterday or today. The commit says the extra day is only needed in the hour after midnight BST, but the tests accept it at every hour of the day. The plants backend stores submittedAt as a LocalDateTime with no zone (NotificationAggregate.java:50), and the confirmation controller reads it with new Date(value) before formatting for Europe/London. The date shown is therefore only right when the frontend process runs in UTC, and the tests cannot catch it being wrong.","scenario":"The plants frontend runs with TZ=Europe/London (native dev, or a container setting changes). At 14:00 a trader submits on 12 October. The zone-less submittedAt is read as London time, so a UTC-to-London regression can push the date a day off. If the page shows 11 October, review.spec.ts:201 and :412 still pass because yesterday is in the accepted set. The same applies to the animals 'Date submitted' header.","severity":"medium","category":"test-weakened","fix":"Accept the previous day only when the London clock is between 00:00 and 01:00 (compute the expected set from the current time), or read submittedAt back through the API and format it in Europe/London to get one expected string. Separately, raise a ticket for the plants backend to serialise submittedAt as an Instant (or with an offset) so the frontend's new Date() does not depend on the process timezone.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. review.spec.ts:201 accepts -1/0/+1 days and :412, :629, plus notification-view-states.spec.ts:40, 183, 266, accept -1/0. Commit 3e7b227 says the extra day is only needed in the hour after midnight BST, but the tests accept it at every hour. The reviewer's scenario understates the problem: the bug is live, not hypothetical. The plants frontend Dockerfile already sets ENV TZ=\"Europe/London\" (Dockerfile:9, :34). The backend stamps submittedAt with zone-less LocalDateTime.now() (NotificationService.java:236, NotificationAggregate.java:50), and confirmation/controller.js:25 parses it with new Date(value). A zone-less ISO date-time is parsed as process-local (London) time, so the page shows the backend JVM's UTC wall date. Between 00:00 and 01:00 BST it shows yesterday. The build widened the test to hide a real product bug, and the controller's own comment ('submittedAt is a UTC instant') is wrong. Fix: serialise submittedAt and created as an Instant or with an offset (or parse with an explicit Z), then tighten the tests to today only, or to a set computed from the London clock.","also":[]},{"rank":4,"ranks":[4],"id":"workspace-tooling-f1","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"Gate tears down and deletes the run's own lease after Docker Desktop quits","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"tim/src/build/gate.js","line":429,"what":"psStackWithDocker (tim/src/exec/stack.js:159) now starts Docker Desktop again whenever docker ps says the daemon is unreachable. In the gate, takeLease calls acquireStack as RUN_HOLDER. The run's lease is state UP, and Docker came back with no containers (the stack sets no restart policies). So acquireDecision returns 'start', startUnderLease rebuilds the stack, and lease.started is true. runE2e's finally block then calls giveBack, and releaseStack runs stop-stack.sh and removes the lease file. The gate cannot tell 'I started a free stack' apart from 'I restarted my own run's stack'. Before this change, an unreachable daemon made takeLease fail. The stage reported it, and the new recoverDownStack in the loop would have taken the lease again.","scenario":"An overnight branch run holds the lease as ibl-…, and a Homebrew cask upgrade quits Docker Desktop. The ladder's `tim build gate --phase e2e --holder ibl-…` starts Docker, rebuilds the stack, runs the e2e rungs green, then stops the stack and deletes the lease. The next stage runs `tim docker lease status`: lease null, up false. By RUN_STACK_RULE that means stackHeld with holder null, and confirmStackHeld believes it (null !== RUN_HOLDER). The run stops as 'stack-held' and preserves the attempt; or, where no stage checks, every later e2e gate cold-starts the stack and tears it down. Between gates the stack has no lease, so another session can take it. The end-of-run release finds nothing to release. The stackDown → recoverDownStack path only fires when a stage reads the lease before any gate has 'healed' it.","severity":"medium","category":"lease-safety","fix":"Make acquireStack return whether the start replaced the holder's own lease (previous?.holder === holder), and have runE2e give the stack back only when the gate took a lease nobody held before (lease.started && !lease.resumedOwnLease). Add a gate test: own UP lease, Docker quit, e2e runs, then assert the lease is still the holder's and the stack is up.","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch. The ladder's e2e gate runs with --holder RUN_HOLDER (increment-build-loop.js:945). takeLease (gate.js:259) calls acquireStack, and stackStatus now goes through psStackWithDocker (stack.js:159), which reopens Docker Desktop instead of throwing. The compose files under docker/stack set no `restart:` policy, so the stack comes back with no containers. acquireDecision (stack-lease.js:146) then returns 'start' for the holder's own UP lease when up is false. startUnderLease returns started:true, gate.js:429 calls giveBack, and releaseStack runs stop-stack.sh and removes the lease file. stack-lease.js and gate.js's lease logic are unchanged in the diff. Before the diff, though, the daemon-down case threw inside takeLease ('Can't tell whether the workspace stack is free') and never reached this path, so the diff is what makes it reachable. Unit and FIT gate phases' port checks now reopen Docker too, so the ladder's e2e gate is usually the first thing to see the down stack. recoverDownStack only runs if a stage reads the lease first. After that, a stage sees lease null and stops as stack-held (confirmStackHeld treats a null holder as not this run's), or every later e2e gate cold-starts and releases the stack. Medium is right: it costs lease safety and run continuity, but no wrong code lands green. Suggested fix: carry 'restarted own lease' from acquireStack and skip giveBack in that case.","also":[]},{"rank":5,"ranks":[5],"id":"backend-fetch-f1","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"Two divergent HTTP paths; the CDP path is no longer exercised by any test environment","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend (identical in trade-imports-plants-frontend)","file":"src/server/common/helpers/call-counts/backend-fetch.js","line":21,"what":"connectBoundedDispatcher() returns undefined whenever HTTP_PROXY is set, so fetchOnce (line 58) splits behaviour by environment. Unit, FIT and the local E2E stack (no HTTP_PROXY: docker/stack sets none) send through npm undici 8.11.2's fetch, with an undici FormData copy and a private Agent. CDP, which injects HTTP_PROXY, sends through Node's built-in fetch, with Node's FormData, through the global ProxyAgent. The comment at lines 17-20 says the fork is needed because built-in fetch 'rejects' npm undici agents. The pre-fix logs show otherwise. inc-109-ladder-e2e-rerun at 20:13Z, before 19a76c54 at 20:26Z, shows built-in fetch already connecting through /home/node/node_modules/undici/lib/core/connect.js with a 10000ms timeout. This is because npm undici's lib/global.js installs its Agent under both 'undici.globalDispatcher.2' and, wrapped in a Dispatcher1Wrapper, the legacy '.1' symbol. So the parallel fetch was never needed. In CDP the change adds nothing: there is no 2s bound, and the retry fires only if the socket to the squid proxy itself fails. A backend the proxy cannot reach comes back as a 502/503 response (http target) or as RequestAbortedError 'Proxy response (503) !== 200 when HTTP Tunneling' (https target), and neither is in CONNECT_ERROR_CODES.","scenario":"A future change to how bodies or headers are built (another FormData field, a stream body, a Blob subclass) passes unit, FIT and local E2E because all of them take the undici path, then breaks only in CDP on the built-in fetch path. The reverse has already happened: 19a76c54 sent every upload as text/plain on the undici path, and the backend refused it with HttpMediaTypeNotSupportedException. d66b1dce had to patch that in, and only the E2E baseline caught it.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Revert backendFetch to `countBackendCall(); return fetch(url, init)`, and drop asUndiciFormData/forUndici and test/setup-backend-fetch.js. If a local connect bound is still wanted, set it in one place without forking fetch: for example, in setup-proxy's no-proxy branch call `setGlobalDispatcher(new Agent({ connect: { timeout } }))` from npm undici, which built-in fetch already honours through the legacy symbol. Gate it behind a stack-only env var, so CDP and the tests run the same fetch and FormData.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. backend-fetch.js:21-26 returns no dispatcher when httpProxy is set, so fetchOnce (line 58) sends CDP calls through built-in fetch, Node FormData and the global ProxyAgent, while unit, FIT and the local stack go through npm undici fetch, an undici FormData copy and a private Agent. The fork was not needed. npm undici's lib/global.js installs its Agent under 'undici.globalDispatcher.2' and also, wrapped in Dispatcher1Wrapper, under the legacy '.1' symbol. The pre-fix log inc-109-ladder-e2e-rerun (20:13:49Z) shows built-in fetch failing inside /home/node/node_modules/undici/lib/core/connect.js with 'timeout: 10000ms'. So setGlobalDispatcher(new Agent({connect:{timeout}})) in a stack-only branch would have bounded the connect without a second fetch path. The 19a76c54 → d66b1dce text/plain upload regression shows this divergence already broke once. Medium is right: no CDP break today, but nothing exercises the CDP path any more.","also":[]},{"rank":6,"ranks":[6],"id":"backend-fetch-f4","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"FIT global timeout raised to fit full-journey set-up repeated per one-page test","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"package.json","line":23,"what":"d1884244 raised --global-timeout from 900000 to 1500000, and the FIT job timeout-minutes from 20 to 30 (.github/workflows/check-pull-request.yml:115), because the suite outgrew 900s. The CI evidence: main run 38217892519 ran 455 tests in 12.7m (762s, already 85% of the old cap). The combined branch run 38219575045 ran 529 tests in 18.0m. Both had 0 flaky and 0 retried tests, so the raise is not hiding flakes. The slowdown is more than the extra tests explain: the average test went from 1.67s to 2.04s, and 20 tests now take 15s or more (1 on main). Every one of those is a one-page assertion whose beforeEach drives sign-in, every answer section, the health certificate upload, submit and the dashboard. Examples: check-answers.fit.spec.js:876-902 ('submitted notification', 9 tests, added in 40c3b6cc/df59fbde), cancel-amend.fit.spec.js:85-89 (submitNotification then amend), and dashboard.fit.spec.js submitted/amending rows.","scenario":"Each new assertion about a submitted or amending notification adds about 16s of repeated journey driving. The 1500s cap is reached after about 25 more such tests, and the next raise follows the same pattern. Meanwhile real per-test slowness has 420s of headroom to grow into without anyone seeing it.","severity":"medium","category":"test-performance","fix":"For these one-page assertions, use a seeded submitted (or amending) notification for set-up, which the house rule allows for single-page assertions. Keep the full submit journey in one journey test. Then set the budget from the measured run (roughly 1.3× runtime) rather than a round 1500s.","verifierNote":"d1884244 raised --global-timeout from 900000 to 1500000 (package.json:23) and the FIT job timeout from 20 to 30 (check-pull-request.yml:115). The 'submitted notification' describe in check-answers.fit.spec.js (around line 876) runs the whole journey in beforeEach: sign-in, every section, the health-certificate upload, submit and the dashboard. It uses a 90s per-test timeout. The pattern existed on main, but only with 2 tests and no upload. The build added several more tests and an upload step, so it multiplied the cost rather than inventing it. I did not re-check the CI run IDs and timings, but the code shape supports the claim. The house rule allows seeding to set up a one-page assertion, so the fix is in policy.","also":[]},{"rank":7,"ranks":[7,9],"id":"animals-fe-shared-f1","area":"Animals frontend shared code","priority":2,"title":"Transit and destination lists now offer subdivision codes that reach GBN-AG as raw country IDs","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/services/countries/index.js","line":121,"what":"destinationCountryOptions() (via countryAndSubdivisionOptions) now offers subdivision codes such as FR-MQ or PT-30 as values, and the transit country search and the import-reason destination select now take their allow-list from it. Origin handles subdivisions in two fields: countryOfOriginSubdivisionCode, mapped by notification-mapper/mapper-a/sections/origin.js to parentCountryCode(code) plus countrySubdivisionCode. Transit has no such split. mapper-a/sections/transport.js sends transitedCountries through unchanged. The backend's SpecifiedConsignment then builds TradeCountry.fromCountryCodes(transport.getTransitedCountries()), which wraps each string as CodedValue.of(code), so the GBN-AG TradeCountry ID becomes 'FR-MQ' instead of an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code. Sam's ruling (2026-10-09, items 26-27) says to offer the subdivisions, but neither inc-006 nor inc-174 looked at the outbound mapping. The inc-006 plan only checked that the backend's destinationCountry is a free string.","scenario":"A trader on a road consignment searches 'Martinique', picks 'Martinique (France)' and submits. The notification stores transitedCountries ['FR-MQ']. The animals backend outbox puts SpecifiedConsignment.transitCountries [{ID: 'FR-MQ'}] in the GBN-AG message. The downstream consumer rejects it or misreads the country, while the same territory chosen as the origin is sent correctly as FR plus subdivision FR-MQ.","severity":"medium","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Settle the contract before release. Either map transited and destination subdivisions the way origin does (parent country code, with the subdivision carried separately if GBN-AG has a field for it) in mapper-a/sections/transport.js and wherever destinationCountry leaves the service, or confirm with the GBN-AG/IPAFFS owners that subdivision codes are valid there. Add a mapper test for a subdivision transit code.","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch. transit-countries/validate.js now builds offeredCodes and offeredCountries from countries.destinationCountryOptions(), which includes subdivision keys such as FR-MQ (countries/index.js countryAndSubdivisionOptions). Before this change transit used originCountries(), which held countries only. mapper-a/sections/transport.js:45 passes transitedCountries through as reader.scalar, and the diff leaves that file untouched. In the backend, TradeCountry.fromCountryCodes (outbox/gbnag/TradeCountry.java:21-27) wraps each string as CodedValue.of(code) with a null subdivision, and SpecifiedConsignment.java:43 sends that list. So a picked subdivision reaches GBN-AG as ID 'FR-MQ', while origin splits the same code into a parent country plus a subdivision. Nothing in the backend checks the codes. On destinationCountry: it goes through direct-fields.js and NotificationCopyMapper only, and I found no GBN-AG outbox use, so the release risk is mainly transit. Medium is right: it is a contract defect that the downstream consumer may reject, and no test covers it.","also":[{"rank":9,"id":"feature-folders-f1","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/transport/transit-countries/validate.js","line":23,"repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","what":"offeredCountries now returns countries.destinationCountryOptions(), which includes subdivisions such as ES-CN. Validation accepts these codes and the controller saves them into transitedCountries; import-reason's destination selects do the same. In the animals backend, SpecifiedConsignment builds the transit list with TradeCountry.fromCountryCodes(transport.getTransitedCountries()), which wraps each code unchanged as CodedValue.of(code). The origin page maps a subdivision back to its parent (parentCountryCode) for the region-code prefix, but nothing on the transit or destination path maps or splits a subdivision. The backlog deliberately asks for the subdivision code to be saved, but no increment checked what the outbound GBN-AG message does with it.","scenario":"A rail consignment adds 'Canary Islands (Spain)' as a transit country, so transitedCountries is ['ES-CN']. When submitted, the GBN-AG payload carries a TradeCountry with code ES-CN where an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code is expected. The downstream system may reject the message or mis-route it, and the frontend fit tests cannot see this.","fix":"Before merge, confirm with the GBN-AG/PIMS mapping (docs/analysis/gbn-ag-field-lineage.md) whether a subdivision code is valid in the transit and destination country fields. If it is not, map it to its parent (and send the subdivision separately if the schema has a slot for it) in the backend outbox mapper, not in the frontend. Add an outbox test with a subdivision code either way.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. transit-countries/validate.js now builds offeredCodes and offeredCountries from countries.destinationCountryOptions(), and the new unit test 'Should commit a subdivision code and continue' saves ['FR','ES-CN']. In the backend, TradeCountry.fromCountryCodes (outbox/gbnag/TradeCountry.java:21) wraps each code unchanged as new TradeCountry(CodedValue.of(code), null) and leaves the subordinateTradeCountrySubDivision slot it has empty. So ES-CN goes out as a country code. The backlog row (arrival-and-transit, 'Save and continue saves its code') asks for this on Sam's 9 Oct ruling, but no row checks the outbound GBN-AG message. One part of the claim is overstated: destinationCountry is not mapped into the gbnag outbox at all (no match in outbox/), so the GBN-AG risk is limited to transit countries. Medium stands because the result is an outbound message to an external system that may reject it, and no test covers it."}]},{"rank":8,"ranks":[8],"id":"ins-frontend-f1","area":"INS frontend","priority":2,"title":"Countries 503 still logs no fetch cause: Boom puts the cause in data, not error.cause","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/app/services/countries/index.js","line":17,"what":"stackWithCauses follows error.cause. The only 5xx Boom this app raises around a fetch is Boom.serverUnavailable('Reference data unavailable', { dataset, cause: err }). @hapi/boom has no cause option, so the fetch error goes into response.data.cause and response.cause stays undefined. catchAll then logs only the Boom stack. The new test builds Boom.boomify(new TypeError('fetch failed', { cause })), a shape this code path never produces.","scenario":"On CDP, reference-data is unreachable (for example the egress proxy or DNS fails) → GET /address-book/add → ensureLoaded throws Boom 503 → catchAll logs 'Error: Reference data unavailable' and its stack, with no 'Caused by:' line. The socket or DNS error this commit was meant to expose is still missing from the log.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Use Boom.boomify(err, { statusCode: 503, message: 'Reference data unavailable', data: { dataset: 'countries' } }), which keeps the original error and its cause chain. Or attach the cause to the Boom (Object.assign(boom, { cause: err })). Add a test that drives ensureLoaded with nock replyWithError and checks the logged 'Caused by:' line.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. countries/index.js:17 (untouched by the diff, but it is the main 5xx path the new errors.js logging is for) calls Boom.serverUnavailable(message, data). @hapi/boom 'serverUnavailable(message, data)' passes data through internals.serverError to options.data (lib/index.js:117-118, 381-383), and boom never sets err.cause, so response.cause is undefined. The dashboard controller rethrows Boom errors (controller.js:114-115), so an unreachable reference-data service reaches catchAll as a 503 and stackWithCauses prints only the Boom stack, with no 'Caused by:'. One correction: the test shape (Boom.boomify of a TypeError with a cause) is not impossible. It is exactly what hapi does to an uncaught non-Boom error thrown from a handler, so the new code does work for uncaught throws. It does not work for the countries outage, which is the one fetch path this app wraps in Boom itself. Fix as suggested: boomify the original error with statusCode 503, or keep the cause on the Boom.","also":[]},{"rank":10,"ranks":[10],"id":"plants-features-f1","area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Outstanding-row error links trap a half-done row in a Check your answers loop","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/high-risk-plants/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/view-model/outstanding-rows.js","line":45,"what":"outstandingRowHref sends every row error to rowEntry(row) (the row's FIRST gate-passing page) wrapped in withChange (?change=1). The pages post back to their own URL (form has no action), and kit.exitTarget turns any ?change=1 save into a redirect to notification-view. When the row is outstanding because a LATER page in it is unanswered, the trader is sent to the first page, which is already answered. Saving there returns them to Check your answers with the same error, and the page that is actually missing is never reached.","scenario":"Plants-for-planting draft with arrivalStatus = not-yet-arrived and no arrivalDate (or a potatoes draft with commodityType set but no commodity lines). Continue on Check your answers gives 'Complete arrival details' (or 'Complete what you are importing'). The link opens /arrival-status?change=1 (or /commodity-type?change=1) with the radio already checked. Save and continue: arrival-status always calls nextTarget, which goes back to /notification-view. commodity-type does the same when no lines are removed. The same error shows again, so the trader loops until they find another way in through Overview. The fit test only covers potatoes, where arrival-status is out of scope, so it misses this. controller.test.js asserts href `${base}/commodity-type?change=1`, which fixes the defect in place.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Link each row error to the first gate-passing page in the row whose collects are not yet fulfilled (use statusOf per page), not to the row's first page. Or keep the change context only for single-page rows and send multi-page rows to the hub row entry without ?change=1. Add a plants/wood test for the arrival row (status answered, date missing) and a test for a typed commodity row with no lines.","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch. outstanding-rows.js:45-48 is new. It links each row error to rowEntry(row) plus ?change=1, and rowEntry (flow/navigation.js:20-23) returns the row's first gate-passing page whatever has already been answered there. Both arrival-status and commodity-type have a form with no action (`<form method=\"post\" novalidate>`), so the POST keeps ?change=1. kit.exitTarget (kit.js:133-137) then sends the trader to CYA_SLUG. arrival-status/controller.js always ends with nextTarget. commodity-type does the same unless lines were removed. The arrival row is [arrivalStatusPage, arrivalDetailsPage] and the commodities row is [commodityTypePage, commoditiesPage, commodityDetailsPage] (task-rows.js:30,33). So a row with its first page answered and a later page missing sends the trader to the answered page and straight back to Check your answers with the same error. controller.test.js:336-346 seeds potatoes with no lines and asserts href `${base}/commodity-type?change=1`, which is exactly this trap. The trader can still get out through Overview or a summary-row Change link, so it is a navigation loop, not data loss. Medium stands.","also":[]},{"rank":11,"ranks":[11],"id":"animals-backend-f3","area":"Animals backend","priority":1,"title":"No IT covers the path that clears action-required, or a clean submit's event","repo":"trade-imports-animals-backend","file":"src/test/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/animals/integration/NotificationIT.java","line":938,"what":"The existing outbox event ITs (around lines 1340, 1444 and 1488) were re-pointed from SUBMITTED to SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED instead of being given a clean ITAHC, and their comments still say 'through SUBMITTED'. As a result, no integration test asserts the normal event a correct submit produces: a clean ITAHC giving notificationStatusCode SUBMITTED. No IT drives the designed recovery path either: SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED, then amend, upload a clean ITAHC, resubmit, then SUBMITTED with outstandingItems [] on the stored aggregate and the NotificationSubmissionAmended event. That path is unit-tested only, with DocumentService mocked.","scenario":"Suppose a regression makes resubmission ignore documents uploaded during an amendment, for example by reading documents for the wrong reference or reading before the upload is persisted. Every IT stays green, and traders are left stuck at 'Submitted action required' after uploading their certificate.","severity":"low","category":"test-coverage","fix":"Add ITs through the real endpoints for both paths. First: submit, amend, givenACleanItahc, submit; assert status SUBMITTED, outstandingItems empty, and the last event's notificationStatusCode. Second: a clean-ITAHC first submit asserting the event's notificationStatusCode is SUBMITTED. Fix the stale 'through SUBMITTED' comments in the re-pointed tests.","verifierNote":"Partly overstated but real. A clean-ITAHC submit IT exists (NotificationIT:867), but it asserts only the aggregate status and outstandingItems. It does not check the outbox event's notificationStatusCode, and the only IT on that field is line 938, which pins SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED. No IT drives the path SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED, then amend, upload a clean ITAHC, resubmit. createAndSubmitNotificationWithFullContent seeds the ITAHC before the first submit. The stale comment at line 1341 ('through SUBMITTED') sits above an assertion of SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED, as the reviewer said. This is a test gap only, so low.","also":[]},{"rank":12,"ranks":[12],"id":"workspace-tooling-f2","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"Failed-attempt handler's soft reset sweeps up commits that are not the attempt's","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":".claude/skills/requirements-pipeline/workflow/increment-build-loop.js","line":1511,"what":"New step 0 of the branch-lifecycle preserve prompt treats every commit in `origin/<branch>..HEAD` as the failed attempt's work. It writes them out as a patch, then runs `reset --soft origin/<branch>`, and later steps stash the result. That rests on 'No stage commits: only landing does, and it pushes at once'. But the loop has a push-failed outcome that leaves a landed, recorded, unpushed commit on the branch by design ('a resume pushes the recorded commit'). The branch baseline (branchBaselinePrompt) never checks the ahead count, and a person's own unpushed commit on the theme branch also passes the clean-tree baseline.","scenario":"Row A lands, its push is rejected because the remote moved, and the run stops push-failed with A's commit SHA recorded. The person relaunches with an explicit id queue, or the derived order puts row B first. B's ladder goes red, and preserveAttempt soft-resets A's commit together with B's work into B's failure stash. A's row still records its commit, so A resumes past build. The find-PRs stage sees nothing ahead to push and marks A done. A's change never reaches the branch, and survives only in logs/<B>-preserve-*.commits.patch.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Either make the baseline refuse to start a row when any configured repo has commits ahead of origin/<branch> (or push them first, as the find-PRs stage does), or have step 0 reset only commits made after a HEAD SHA the loop records at the start of the row's baseline.","verifierNote":"Step 0 (increment-build-loop.js:1510-1517, added in 4d734946) does soft-reset everything in origin/<branch>..HEAD in every configured repo. The push-failed outcome (4363-4371) does leave a recorded, unpushed commit, and branchBaselinePrompt (2848) checks only the branch and clean trees, never the ahead count. So the scenario can happen. It needs three things: a push-failed stop, a relaunch before the human reconciles the branch (which the code comment expects them to do), and a different row's red ladder before A is pushed. A different row comes first only with an explicit id queue or a derived order that puts B first. Nothing is lost outright: the commits go to logs/<B>-preserve-*.commits.patch and the note names their SHAs. A's row would still show a commit that is not on the branch, though, which is a silent integrity gap. The scenario is narrow and the work can be recovered, so low rather than medium. The fix the reviewer suggests (refuse at baseline when ahead > 0, or record a per-row HEAD and reset only past it) is sound.","also":[]},{"rank":13,"ranks":[13],"id":"workspace-tooling-f3","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"Theme spec check stops the run on gaps that predate the theme, and on prefixes the theme never touched","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":".claude/skills/requirements-pipeline/workflow/increment-build-loop.js","line":3253,"what":"themeSpecCheck turns any `tim spec gaps --none` noneCount > 0, or any lint finding, across the whole prefix into a 'spec-check-red' stop. That includes gaps that predate the theme. It also adds every prefix reachable from the repos of theme rows this run did not build. A row with no `repos` counts as touching every configured repo, and animals-backend maps to both live-animals and germinal-products.","scenario":"A theme was built over two launches, and its earlier rows changed only trade-imports-animals-backend for live animals. When the last row lands, the 'repo reach' source adds germinal-products/. That prefix has unproven scenarios from before this programme, so the run stops with SPEC CHECK RED and prescribes 'catch-up and cover germinal' for a set the theme never touched.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Compare against a per-prefix gaps baseline taken at the run's (or theme's) first baseline and stop only on new noneScenarios. For rows not built in this run, use the prefixes recorded in their landed Spec sync lines, which are already parsed, instead of repo reach.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. prefixesBySource (3243-3250) adds 'repo reach' for rows this run did not build. SPEC_PREFIXES_BY_REPO_PATH (753-762) maps both animals repos to ['live-animals','germinal-products']. gapsProblem (3209) turns any noneCount > 0 into a problem, and themeSpecCheck stops the run with spec-check-red. A theme built over two launches can therefore stop on germinal-products gaps it never touched, and the run prescribes 'catch-up and cover germinal'. Stopping on gaps that predate the theme within prefixes the theme did touch is arguably the intended strict policy, given Sam's 'failures are mine to fix' stance. The repo-reach over-inclusion is the real defect. The effect is a false stop at the end of a theme with a clear message, not wrong code, so low. The better fix is to use the recorded landed Spec sync prefixes for rows not built in this run.","also":[]},{"rank":14,"ranks":[14],"id":"workspace-tooling-f5","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"FIT deny rule blocks only test:fit, not test:fit:features or test:fit:journeys","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":".claude/skills/requirements-pipeline/workflow/increment-build-loop.js","line":1364,"what":"FIT_ON_STACK_PORT = /\\brun test:fit(?!:)/ is meant to refuse plan checks that serve a frontend on the stack's port. The negative lookahead lets any `test:fit:<suffix>` through. In animals-frontend and plants-frontend, `test:fit:features` and `test:fit:journeys` call `npm run test:fit` with no PORT, and playwright.config.js sets reuseExistingServer: false.","scenario":"A planner writes `npm --prefix ~/…/trade-imports-plants-frontend run test:fit:features -- tests/x.spec.js` as a section-5 check. The deny list accepts it, the webServer can't bind 3003 (the leased stack holds it), and the ladder goes red, preserving and stopping a correct increment.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Deny every test:fit form except test:fit:ci, e.g. /\\brun test:fit(?!:ci\\b)/, and add a contract test case for test:fit:features.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. FIT_ON_STACK_PORT = /\\brun test:fit(?!:)/ (line 1364) lets test:fit:features and test:fit:journeys through. In both frontends these scripts call `npm run test:fit` with no PORT. plants playwright.config.js uses PORT ?? 3003 with reuseExistingServer: false, so on a leased stack holding that port the webServer cannot start. A planner who writes test:fit:features as a section-5 check gets past the deny list, and the ladder goes red with nothing broken. Low, because the effect is a false red stop, which preserves the work. The fix /\\brun test:fit(?!:ci\\b)/ plus a contract test is right.","also":[]},{"rank":15,"ranks":[15],"id":"workspace-tooling-f4","area":"Workspace tooling","priority":1,"title":"Read-only stack queries now launch Docker Desktop and can block for 5 minutes","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"tim/src/exec/stack.js","line":159,"what":"Every caller of stackContainers or stackContainerIds now goes through psStackWithDocker: lease status, gate held-port checks, writeStackLogs, recordLeaseContainers. On macOS that runs `open -a Docker` and polls for up to TIM_DOCKER_START_WAIT_MS (default 5 minutes) whenever the daemon is unreachable. On a machine whose docker context is Colima or OrbStack, the daemon-unreachable stderr matches but `open -a Docker` fails. The user then sees 'Docker Desktop would not open' instead of the real cause.","scenario":"Someone runs `tim docker lease status` to check who holds the stack while Docker is deliberately stopped. Docker Desktop opens and the command hangs for up to 5 minutes. withStackLogs after a red e2e rung can stall the same way.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Restart Docker only on the start paths (run-stack.sh and acquire). Let status reads report 'Docker not running' as up:false with a reason. Check `docker context show` before assuming Docker Desktop.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. stackStatus → stackContainerIds → psStackWithDocker → ensureDockerRunning (docker-daemon.js:81) runs `open -a Docker` and polls for up to TIM_DOCKER_START_WAIT_MS (5 minutes by default). So `tim docker lease status`, the gate's port checks and writeStackLogs all launch Docker Desktop as a side effect of a read. The 5-minute hang happens only when Desktop opens but never answers; a normal start takes well under a minute. The Colima and OrbStack case is real but hypothetical on this team, which runs Docker Desktop. It is a design wart rather than a breaking bug. It also feeds f1, because it makes the stack read heal the daemon silently. Low.","also":[]},{"rank":16,"ranks":[16],"id":"perf-f2","area":"Performance tests","priority":1,"title":"Missed-dashboard warning is logged on every VU iteration and includes the OIDC callback URL with its auth code","repo":"trade-imports-performance-tests","file":"src/k6/front-door.js","line":64,"what":"openInsDashboard calls console.warn(describeMissedPage(...)) on every failed dashboard check, on the hot path of dashboardOnlySession, addressBookSession and every journey session. Every other console call in src/k6 and src/suites runs in setup or teardown. describeMissedPage puts the full page.url in the line. The commit's own test shows this is the /auth/sign-in-oidc?code=... callback when sign-in fails.","scenario":"Defra ID or the INS session store degrades during a peak-day or spike run on CDP. Every dashboard-opening session, hundreds a minute, writes a warn line holding the authorisation code from the callback URL. The k6 log fills with near-identical lines and hides the setup and teardown summary lines. The codes end up in CDP's log store. They are short-lived and come from the stub, but it is a bad pattern to copy across to real identity providers.","severity":"low","category":"security","fix":"Strip the query string before logging (pathOf in pages.js already does this). Log once per VU, or add to a Counter tagged with status and heading so the summary shows what the missed page was without one line per iteration.","verifierNote":"Confirmed in src/k6/front-door.js:61-65. console.warn(describeMissedPage(...)) runs on every failed dashboard check inside openInsDashboard, which is on the per-session hot path. describeMissedPage puts in the full url, query string included, and the commit's own test case shows a /auth/sign-in-oidc?code=... URL. The reviewer is slightly overstated in saying every other console call is in setup or teardown: call-counts.js and resilience.js also log at run time. Still, nothing else logs once per iteration on a failure path. The code reaching the log has already been spent on the failed callback, and in perf it comes from the Defra ID stub, so the security risk is minimal. The flooding during a degraded run is real. Fix: strip the query with pathOf, and either log once per VU or count failures with a tagged Counter.","also":[]},{"rank":17,"ranks":[17],"id":"perf-f1","area":"Performance tests","priority":1,"title":"k6 live-animals walk still out of step with the real opening run after additional-details; cph-number is no longer a run step","repo":"trade-imports-performance-tests","file":"src/k6/live-animals.js","line":208,"what":"The frontend's RUN_STEPS (animals-frontend src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/flow/run.js) now runs origin, commodities, import-reason, consignment-details, identification, additional-details, port-of-entry, transit-countries, transporters, documents, addresses (endsRunWhenComplete), contact, notification-view. This change also removed cph-number from the run: it is reached only from its row on the addresses page. The k6 draft fixes only the first six steps. It still walks additional-details, documents, addresses, cph-number, port-of-entry, transit-countries, transporters, contact. walker.reach (src/k6/pages.js) throws away the page a save landed on when its path differs from the next step's, and opens the expected page directly. The `saved` check only tests status 200, no stale redirect and no redirect loop. So every mismatch costs a GET a real user never makes, and nothing fails. The new live-animals.test.js claims the order is 'as Design Release 2.1 orders them' but pins only names.slice(0, 6), so the wrong tail is locked in, not caught.","scenario":"A single-session live-animals notification during peak-day. (1) The additional-details save lands on /port-of-entry; k6 discards it and GETs /documents. (2) The addresses save lands on /consignment/contact/select, or on the hub when nothing is outstanding; k6 GETs /cph-number, a page the run no longer visits. (3) The cph-number save falls through nextInTaskRow back to /addresses; k6 GETs /port-of-entry. (4) The transporters save lands on /documents; k6 GETs /consignment/contact/select. That is 4 extra page renders per notification, about 2 of them new since the opening-run change. Each is counted in page_requests and run.counter. reEditUntilTarget then cuts the padding re-edits to keep pagesPerNotification, so the per-endpoint mix (documents, cph-number, port-of-entry and contact GETs up, re-edit saves down) drifts from the traffic model. Every check stays green.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Reorder the draft tail to match RUN_STEPS: additional-details, port-of-entry, transit-countries, transporters, documents, addresses, contact. Model cph-number as a detour inside the addresses step: open it from the addresses page, save it, land back on addresses, then save addresses. Drop it from its own top-level slot. Change live-animals.test.js to assert the full draft order. Better still, make reach record a counter or warn (reuse describeMissedPage) when, during the opening run, it has to open a page directly instead of landing on it, so the next frontend reorder fails loudly.","verifierNote":"Confirmed on the branch. The animals-frontend diff to flow/run.js removes cphNumberPage from RUN_STEPS and adds endsRunWhenComplete on addresses. The k6 draft in src/k6/live-animals.js (lines 208-218) still walks documents, addresses, cph-number, port-of-entry, transit-countries, transporters, contact. walker.reach in src/k6/pages.js:150 keeps the landed page only when the status is 200 and the path matches. Otherwise it opens the expected path directly, and saved() only checks isSaved. Mismatches therefore cost extra GETs and nothing fails. live-animals.test.js pins only names.slice(0, 6). I am downgrading it from medium. Most of the tail mismatch was already on main, since main's frontend also ran port-of-entry before documents. The only new drift is addresses → cph-number: main's run went from addresses to cph-number, and now it goes to contact. That is about one extra direct GET per notification. Real users still open cph-number from its row on the addresses page, so it stays a real page in the mix. Thresholds and measurements are unchanged, so this is a small skew in the traffic model, not a broken measurement. The suggested fix is sound: reorder the tail, make cph-number a detour from addresses, assert the full order, and warn or count when reach falls back mid-run.","also":[]},{"rank":18,"ranks":[18],"id":"stub-ref-f3","area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"title":"An unrecognised MDM traffic value silently becomes type null and sorts last","repo":"trade-imports-reference-data","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/portsofentry/PortType.java","line":42,"what":"fromTraffic matches 'Airport', 'Port' and 'Rail' exactly, ignoring case only. It does not trim, and no log or metric records a miss. PortOfEntry maps a miss to null, and PortListOrder puts null types after rail. Every observed value is one of the three, but only CDP-test MDM was captured, not production, and MDM's data is known to be messy (duplicates, 'Teestort', non-breaking spaces).","scenario":"Production MDM returns traffic 'Port ' (trailing space), 'Seaport', 'Sea Port' or 'Airport/Port' for a real port. reference-data serves it with \"type\":null after Folkestone, below every rail port. A user scanning the seaports A to Z does not find it, and nothing in the logs shows that MDM's vocabulary has changed.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Strip the traffic value before matching. Log a warning once per unrecognised value (code plus raw traffic) when a PortOfEntry is built, so production drift shows up. Optionally capture production or pre-production MDM traffic values before release.","verifierNote":"Confirmed in PortType.fromTraffic (lines 41-48). It uses equalsIgnoreCase with no strip() and logs nothing on a miss. The test keepsAPortOfUnknownTypeLast pins a null type that sorts last. Every captured value matches one of the three, so the trailing-space or 'Seaport' scenario is hypothetical drift in production MDM, not a current fault. Low, and the fix is cheap (strip the value and log a warning on a miss).","also":[]},{"rank":19,"ranks":[19],"id":"stub-ref-f2","area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"title":"The stub's blocks filter is presented as MDM's behaviour, but the evidence also fits another explanation","repo":"trade-imports-stub","file":"src/main/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/stubs/mdm/countries/CountriesController.java","line":28,"what":"The Javadoc says blocks 'keeps the records that carry that block whatever includeCountry says, as MDM does', and carriesBlock looks only at the country-level blocks[].name. The accepted spec (stub-tidy-up-spec.md:48-49) admits a second explanation: GB may come back because of its GB-NIR subdivision bloc. GB's own 'GBNAG_SPS_EX' block name is also an inference: the jq program renamed every masked '******_EX' token. 35 other block entries have no name at all (masked), so filtering on any block other than GBNAG_SPS_EX returns []. Matching is exact and case-sensitive. Real MDM's behaviour for an unknown block, a comma-separated list or different case is unknown, but [] for an unknown block is now pinned by a test.","scenario":"A future consumer asks for blocks=EU, or for a block that a country reaches only through a subdivision bloc. The stub returns [] or a different set from real MDM, and local and CDP-dev tests pass against behaviour that production will not show. Nothing breaks today: reference-data is the only caller, it keeps only includeCountry=true and it drops GB.","severity":"low","category":"stub-fidelity","fix":"Change the Javadoc and the 'High fidelity' doc row to say what was observed (one SPS capture) and that unnamed blocks are not filterable. Confirm the selection rule with the MDM team, or with one more capture of a block reached only through a subdivision, before relying on it.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. The CountriesController Javadoc (lines 28-31) says 'as MDM does'. stub-tidy-up-spec.md:48-49 itself gives a second explanation (GB could come back through its GB-NIR bloc), and line 79 says the masked block names were left out and GBNAG_SPS_EX was used for the proven entries. carriesBlock is an exact, case-sensitive match on the country-level blocks[].name only. Nothing breaks today: the only caller is reference-data, and it asks for GBNAG_SPS_EX alone (the fixture has 32 matches). The risk is the Javadoc and the fidelity row overclaiming, so low is right.","also":[]},{"rank":20,"ranks":[20],"id":"stub-ref-f4","area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"title":"New reference-data controller test injects mocks into the controller and mocks an internal service","repo":"trade-imports-reference-data","file":"src/test/java/uk/gov/defra/trade/imports/portsofentry/PortsOfEntryControllerTest.java","line":17,"what":"The test uses @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension) with @InjectMocks on the controller and mocks MdmService, an in-process collaborator, not the HTTP boundary. docs/best-practices/java/testing/unit.md says never to use @InjectMocks and to test controllers with @WebMvcTest, and Sam's rule is to mock only at the network boundary. The edge cases (null or unknown traffic giving \"type\":null on the wire, non-breaking spaces, ties broken by code, case-insensitive matching) are proved only on Java objects, never through Feign decoding and Jackson serialisation. The WireMock IT covers six well-formed ports.","scenario":"Someone later adds @JsonInclude(NON_NULL), or changes PortType's @JsonValue or deserialisation. Unknown-type ports then lose their type key, or the frontend gets a different wire shape. Every unit test stays green because none of them serialises, and the IT has no unknown-traffic or missing-traffic port to catch it.","severity":"low","category":"test-behaviour","fix":"Add a port with unknown traffic, one with no traffic and one with a non-breaking space to src/test/resources/integration/mdm-poe-response.json, and assert the order and the \"type\":null wire shape in PortsOfEntryControllerIT. Test PortListOrder and PortType directly as pure units, with no mocks, and drop the @InjectMocks controller test.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. PortsOfEntryControllerTest uses @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension), @Mock MdmService and @InjectMocks on the controller. docs/best-practices/java/testing/unit.md:42 says 'Never use @InjectMocks', and the test mocks an in-process service, not the network boundary. Every edge case (unknown or null traffic, NBSP, tie on code, case) is asserted only on Java objects. None of them goes through Feign decoding or the JSON wire shape (\"type\":null). The breakage scenario is a later regression slipping through, not a present bug, so low.","also":[]},{"rank":21,"ranks":[21],"id":"stub-ref-f1","area":"Stub and reference-data","priority":1,"title":"The jq programs that rebuild the stub fixtures, and the stub doc, are not committed","repo":"trade-imports-workspace","file":"workareas/shared/design-release-2-1/mdm/regenerate/countries.jq","line":1,"what":"The 256 KB countries fixture and the ports fixture can only be rebuilt from the raw masked captures by workareas/shared/design-release-2-1/mdm/regenerate/{countries,ports}.jq. `git status` shows that folder as untracked (??). The docs/repos/trade-imports-stub.md edit that names those programs and raises the stub's fidelity from 'Shape only' to 'High' is uncommitted (' M'). The plan said 'the orchestrator commits the workspace', but nothing was committed. The stub repo's own README and the commit say nothing about where the fixtures came from.","scenario":"Someone resets or cleans the workspace checkout, or reviews only the stub PR. The jq programs are lost, and with them the only record of which masked fields were left out and which were recovered from cdp-test captures (effectiveAlias, the GBNAG_SPS_EX block names, GB MID's spaces, the townCity values). MDM changes, and nobody can regenerate the fixture the same way. The next person hand-edits 10k lines or recaptures without the masking rules.","severity":"low","category":"provenance","fix":"Commit regenerate/*.jq and the stub doc edit on the workspace branch of the same name. Add one line to the stub README (or to the commit history the PR carries) pointing to the captures and the regenerate programs.","verifierNote":"Confirmed: `git status` shows workareas/shared/design-release-2-1/mdm/regenerate/ as untracked and docs/repos/trade-imports-stub.md as modified but not staged. Nothing breaks at runtime. The fixtures are committed in the stub repo and the raw captures are tracked, so regenerating them would be harder but still possible. This is a handover task (commit the programs and the doc on the workspace branch), not a code defect, so I have lowered it to low.","also":[]},{"rank":22,"ranks":[22],"id":"tests-repo-f3","area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"title":"Negative check on the review runs before the review page has loaded","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/additional-details-scope.spec.ts","line":87,"what":"After `reviewAndSubmitButton.click()` the test immediately runs `expect(getByText('Includes unweaned animals')).toHaveCount(0)` without waiting for the review heading. The Overview never shows that text, so the assertion passes at once while the browser is still on the Overview. As written it can never fail.","scenario":"The commodity change stops clearing the unweaned answer, so the review still shows the 'Includes unweaned animals: Yes' row. The check at line 87 runs before navigation finishes and passes on the Overview. Only the later re-open check (radios unchecked) would catch the regression, and only if the review row and the stored answer go wrong together.","severity":"low","category":"test-weakened","fix":"Wait for the review first (`await expect(animalsPages.notificationView.heading).toBeVisible()` plus a URL check for /notification-view$), then run the toHaveCount(0) check. Better still, check that the 'Additional details' card's terms are exactly the expected list.","verifierNote":"Confirmed at additional-details-scope.spec.ts:86-87. The reviewAndSubmitButton.click() is followed straight away by toHaveCount(0), with no wait for the review heading or URL. The Overview does not carry that text, so the check can pass before navigation finishes. The impact is smaller than claimed: if the stored answer were not cleared, the re-open check at :96 (Yes not checked after cattle is chosen again) would catch it. Only a regression where the review still shows the row while the stored answer is cleared would go unnoticed. Fix as suggested: wait for the review URL and heading first.","also":[]},{"rank":23,"ranks":[23],"id":"tests-repo-f5","area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"title":"Reference-list answer tests still accept any next page after the merge","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/reference-lists/destination-country-list.spec.ts","line":48,"what":"Commit f510245 loosened the post-save check to `url.pathname !== importReason url`, 'whatever page comes next'. That let the reference-lists theme pass both with and without journey-foundation underneath. On the combined branch the destination is settled: a reason opened from its Overview task saves back to the Overview. The loose predicate is still there at destination-country-list.spec.ts:48 and :69 and port-of-exit-list.spec.ts:52. The test at :69 also no longer checks that there is no error summary.","scenario":"A regression makes Save and continue on Main import reason go to an error page or the wrong task. The URL changes, so the predicate passes. The test then opens the Overview itself and checks only the stored value, so the wrong navigation goes unnoticed in these specs.","severity":"low","category":"test-weakened","fix":"Now that all themes are merged, change these to `await expect(animalsPages.overview.heading).toBeVisible()` plus the 'There is a problem' toHaveCount(0) check. That matches what reason-purpose-scope.spec.ts already asserts for the same save.","verifierNote":"Confirmed at destination-country-list.spec.ts:48 and :69 (a new file on the branch). The predicate only requires the pathname to differ from the import-reason page. A server error re-rendered at the same POST path would still fail it, so the reviewer's 'error page' example is weaker than stated. A misroute to the wrong task would pass, though, and :69 has no 'There is a problem' check. The tests' main intent (the answer is stored and re-selected) is still covered, and reason-purpose-scope.spec covers the navigation. Tightening to overview.heading plus the error-summary check is cheap now that the themes are merged.","also":[]},{"rank":24,"ranks":[24],"id":"tests-repo-f6","area":"Tests repo","priority":1,"title":"Verdict on 65b6afc: superseded, not needed, safe to delete","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/animals/e2e/features/promoted-documents.spec.ts","line":39,"what":"65b6afc exists only on backup/inc-107-leaked-commit; no other branch contains it, and none of its content is on the combined branch. It adds: (1) the 'reads each saved document back on the review in its own Document card' test, using references PWA/PWB and a new 250-byte PNG; (2) a new tracked fixture resources/file-upload/safe-file-250b.png plus a `.gitignore` negation `!resources/file-upload/*.png`; (3) a rename of `PW-TXT-` to `PWTXT` in the unsupported-file-type test. Commit 02dd6bc (inc-107, on the combined branch) landed the same test with alphanumeric references PWDOCA/PWDOCB and the existing safeFile100kbJpg fixture, so items 1 and 2 are not needed. Item 3 is not needed either: the frontend's file-type check (documents/form/errors.js fileErrors) rejects the .txt before the backend's `@Pattern(^[a-zA-Z0-9]*$)` on documentReference is reached, so the hyphenated reference does not change which error appears.","scenario":"Cherry-picking 65b6afc onto the combined branch would add a second copy of the document-card test under the same title, which conflicts with 02dd6bc. It would also widen .gitignore to track PNGs under resources/file-upload/ for no current user.","severity":"low","category":"stray-commit","fix":"Delete backup/inc-107-leaked-commit; nothing needs carrying over. Optionally change the unsupported-file-type test (promoted-documents.spec.ts:91) to assert the file-type message rather than the generic 'There is a problem', so it cannot pass because of a different error.","verifierNote":"This is a housekeeping verdict, not a code defect. Checked: `git branch -a --contains 65b6afc` shows only backup/inc-107-leaked-commit. promoted-documents.spec.ts:39 on the combined branch already has the document-card test with PWDOCA/PWDOCB. The unsupported-file test still uses PW-TXT- (:88). As claimed, the animals frontend's documentAddErrors runs fileErrors (FILE_TYPE_MESSAGE for a .txt) locally, so the hyphenated reference never reaches the backend's @Pattern. Deleting the backup branch is safe. Tightening :91 to the file-type message is a good optional improvement.","also":[]},{"rank":25,"ranks":[25],"id":"backend-fetch-f3","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"ETIMEDOUT in the retry set is not guaranteed to mean the request was never sent","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend (identical in trade-imports-plants-frontend)","file":"src/server/common/helpers/call-counts/backend-fetch.js","line":14,"what":"The retry runs for every method, including POST /notifications (create), /{id}/copy, /{id}/submit, /amend, /soft-delete, /notifications/{id}/document-uploads and /document-uploads/{id}/file. It fires if any cause in the chain has UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT, ECONNREFUSED or ETIMEDOUT. The first two can only happen before any request bytes are sent, including during the TLS handshake for an https target, so retrying on them is safe. ETIMEDOUT can also be raised on an established keep-alive socket, from a kernel keepalive or retransmission failure after the request has been written. The logs only show ETIMEDOUT nested under UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT (AggregateError from internalConnectMultiple), so it adds no coverage.","scenario":"POST /notifications/{id}/submit is written on a reused keep-alive socket and the backend commits it. The path then drops before the response arrives and the socket errors with ETIMEDOUT. backendFetch sends the submit again, and the second call gets a 409 or state error, or (on create or copy) a duplicate notification. Unlikely within undici's 300s headersTimeout, but possible, and nothing guards against it.","severity":"low","category":"idempotency","fix":"If the retry survives, limit CONNECT_ERROR_CODES to UND_ERR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT and ECONNREFUSED, and preferably retry only GET/PUT/DELETE, or require an idempotency key for POST.","verifierNote":"ETIMEDOUT is in CONNECT_ERROR_CODES at line 14, and isConnectError walks the whole cause chain for every method, including POST submit, create and copy. undici's h1 client passes a socket error on an established socket straight to in-flight requests as the raw error, unless it is ECONNRESET at message end. So a kernel ETIMEDOUT after the request was written would be retried. The unit test at backend-fetch.test.js:110 shows the intent is not to resend once the connection has opened, and ETIMEDOUT contradicts that intent. It is rare and needs a mid-flight path drop. Low.","also":[]},{"rank":26,"ranks":[26],"id":"backend-fetch-f2","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"Unit tests cannot see what is actually sent; the real-connection tests pass with the feature removed","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend (identical in trade-imports-plants-frontend)","file":"test/setup-backend-fetch.js","line":9,"what":"A global setupFiles vi.mock('undici') replaces undici.fetch with a shim that strips `dispatcher` and converts undici FormData back to Node FormData before calling the mocked global fetch. That mocks a library and rewrites its arguments, rather than mocking at the network boundary. Every unit test of a backend call therefore sees a request shape production never sends, and the conversion code in backend-fetch.js (forUndici/asUndiciFormData) is not observable to them. In backend-fetch.real.test.js, 'tries a refused connection once more' (line 104) only asserts the final ECONNREFUSED, which it gets with or without a retry. 'reaches the backend through its connect-bounded agent' (line 18) passes with the agent removed. No test exercises the 2s bound.","scenario":"Delete the retry branch (lines 74-78) or the `dispatcher` from forUndici: every real-connection test still passes. A FormData conversion bug like the one 19a76c54 shipped passes the whole unit suite, because the shim reconverts the body before any assertion sees it.","severity":"low","category":"test-weakened","fix":"If the code is removed (f1), delete the shim and the real test. If any of it is kept, test only against a real local server: count 'connection' events to prove exactly two attempts, and use a listener that accepts the TCP connection but never answers TLS, or MockAgent, to prove the time bound. Do not mock undici globally in setupFiles.","verifierNote":"Partly real. The setupFiles vi.mock('undici') in test/setup-backend-fetch.js:9-26 strips the dispatcher and converts undici FormData back to Node FormData. Unit tests therefore cannot see the agent or the FormData conversion, and it mocks a library rather than the network boundary. In the real-connection tests, test 1 passes with no agent and test 4 (line 104) asserts only the final ECONNREFUSED, so neither proves a second attempt. Nothing tests the 2s bound. The scenario overstates one point: deleting the retry branch at lines 74-78 WOULD fail backend-fetch.test.js:73-89, which drives the retry through the shim. The FormData gap is now partly closed by the real multipart test at lines 37-77. What remains is an unproven timeout and agent, plus a library-level mock, so low rather than medium.","also":[]},{"rank":27,"ranks":[27],"id":"backend-fetch-f5","area":"Backend fetch timeout and retry","priority":2,"title":"Explanatory comments added against the house rule","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend (identical in trade-imports-plants-frontend)","file":"src/server/common/helpers/call-counts/backend-fetch.js","line":6,"what":"Lines 6-7, 10, 17-20 and 43-44, plus the 6-line header in test/setup-backend-fetch.js, explain the rationale (Docker Desktop forwarder, why there are two fetches, why FormData is copied). The house rule is no explanatory comments; rationale belongs in docs and git history. The comment at 17-20 is also factually misleading (see f1).","scenario":"A reader trusts lines 17-20 ('Node's built-in fetch cannot take an agent from the undici package') and keeps the fork, when the logs show built-in fetch already running on npm undici's Agent.","severity":"low","category":"stray-comment","fix":"Removed along with the code under f1. If anything is kept, move the rationale into the commit message or docs/repos/<repo>.md.","verifierNote":"Lines 6-7, 10, 17-20 and 43-44 and the 6-line header in test/setup-backend-fetch.js are rationale comments, which break the no-explanatory-comments rule. One nuance: lines 17-20 may be literally true for passing an npm undici Agent as a per-call 'dispatcher' to built-in fetch, because of the v1/v2 handler API split. They still mislead, since the global-dispatcher route works, as the 20:13Z log proves.","also":[]},{"rank":28,"ranks":[28,31],"id":"animals-fe-shared-f3","area":"Animals frontend shared code","priority":2,"title":"Amend 'Save and return' skips required checks on import reason only","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/shared/kit.js","line":151,"what":"skipsRequiredChecks is documented as the rule for 'Save and return to overview' and, while amending, 'Save and return' to the review. Only the import-reason controller calls it. Origin, port of entry, transit countries, transporters and additional details pass allowMissing: kit.isHubExit(request). The new save-actions macro gives every amend page the same three controls, but on those five pages amend 'Save and return' to the review still runs the full required checks. The inc-022 plan narrowed this on purpose ('another row's decision'), but no backlog row picks it up, so the controls look the same and behave differently from page to page.","scenario":"A trader amending a submitted notification opens Arrival details from a Change link, clears the arrival time and presses 'Save and return'. The page refuses with the required-time error. On Main import reason, the same action with a blank port of exit saves and returns to the review.","severity":"low","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Decide the rule once. Either switch every soft-save call site to kit.skipsRequiredChecks(request, journey) and add one E2E amend case per page, or record in the backlog or a ruling that amend 'Save and return' keeps required checks everywhere except import reason, and narrow the helper's doc comment to match.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. kit.js:149-151 documents skipsRequiredChecks as covering amend 'Save and return' to the review, but only import-reason/controller.js:101 calls it. Origin (88), port-of-entry (102), transit-countries (153), transporters (144) and additional-details (80) pass allowMissing: kit.isHubExit(request), so an amend save from a Change link still runs the required checks on those pages. The controls are shared, so the same action behaves differently from page to page. No data-integrity risk: the stricter pages fail closed, and submit stays gated. It is a UX inconsistency between the doc comment and the call sites, so low.","also":[{"rank":31,"id":"feature-folders-f2","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/import-reason/controller.js","line":101,"repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","what":"kit.skipsRequiredChecks is documented as true for 'Save and return to overview' and for 'Save and return' to the review while amending. Only import-reason uses it. Origin (origin/controller.js:88), port-of-entry, transit-countries, transporters and additional-details pass allowMissing: kit.isHubExit(request), so the same amend 'Save and return' button runs the required checks there. The same control behaves differently from page to page.","scenario":"While amending, the trader opens Main import reason from a Change link (?change=1), leaves the reason blank and presses 'Save and return': it saves and goes back to the amend review. They then open Arrival details from a Change link, leave the required means of transport unanswered and press 'Save and return': the page refuses with 'Select a means of transport to the port of entry'.","fix":"Decide which behaviour inc-004 and c-211 intend for amend 'Save and return', then use the same predicate on every page that renders amendFormActions: skipsRequiredChecks everywhere, or isHubExit on import-reason too. Add one controller test per page for the amend return path.","verifierNote":"Confirmed in code. The amend 'Save and return' button posts to ?change=1 with no exit=hub (shared/amend-form-actions.js, save-actions.njk). kit.skipsRequiredChecks is used only in import-reason/controller.js:101. Origin (88), port-of-entry (102), transit-countries (153), transporters (144) and additional-details (80) use isHubExit, so on those pages the same button runs the required checks. However, the difference was decided on purpose: inc-022 plan decision 5 says 'use it in the import reason controller only… the other pages' amend behaviour is another row's decision'. The behaviour differs from page to page as the reviewer describes, but nothing is lost or corrupted, and it is a planned deferral rather than a slip. I lowered it to low and suggest tracking it as the missing follow-up row."}]},{"rank":29,"ranks":[29],"id":"ins-frontend-f2","area":"INS frontend","priority":2,"title":"Controller catch blocks still log fetch failures without their causes","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/common/helpers/errors.js","line":21,"what":"The commit says it fixes 'A 500 from a failed backend call logged only TypeError: fetch failed'. But the dashboard and every address-book controller catch non-Boom errors themselves, log them with logger.error({ err }, ...) and return a 500 view. Those errors never reach catchAll. The logger uses @elastic/ecs-pino-format, whose error formatter writes only err.stack as error.stack_trace (checked in node_modules/@elastic/ecs-helpers/lib/error-formatters.js), so the causes are still dropped there.","scenario":"ins-backend times out → GET / → listNotifications rejects with TypeError('fetch failed', { cause: ConnectTimeoutError }) → dashboard/controller.js:117 logs { err } → the ECS line holds only 'TypeError: fetch failed', with no host or connect-timeout detail. This is the same diagnostic gap the commit claims to close.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Add the causes at the logger instead of in catchAll. For example, add a pino err serializer or formatter in logger-options.js that writes stackWithCauses(err) into error.stack_trace. Then catchAll can log { err: response } like every other call site, which also follows the pino-logging best practice of always logging { err }.","verifierNote":"Factually right. Every controller catch block logs logger.error({ err }, ...) (dashboard/controller.js:117, add/edit/delete/list/view controllers). @elastic/ecs-helpers formatError writes only err.stack into error.stack_trace, and logger-options.js adds no err serializer, so causes are dropped there and those errors never reach catchAll. The commit message ('A 500 from a failed backend call logged only TypeError: fetch failed') therefore claims more than it fixes. Severity lowered to low: the catch blocks predate the diff, nothing regressed, and the gap only costs diagnostics. The dashboard path renders a recoverable-error view rather than a 500. Moving cause handling into a pino err serializer is the right fix and would also cover f1's catchAll path.","also":[]},{"rank":30,"ranks":[30],"id":"ins-frontend-f5","area":"INS frontend","priority":2,"title":"Fixture test asserts static data, and the exact count breaks on every recapture","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/app/services/_capture/fixtures.test.js","line":19,"what":"fixtures.test.js checks the contents of a checked-in JSON file: 249 entries, AF first, Aland Islands second, JE present. No app behaviour is tested. The test only fails when someone runs npm run capture:reference-data and MDM has changed, which is the refresh the script exists for.","scenario":"MDM adds or renames a country → a developer recaptures → toHaveLength(249) or the order assertions fail, and the test gets 'fixed' by editing the numbers. The test protects nothing.","severity":"low","category":"test-weakened","fix":"Delete the count and order assertions. If a guard is wanted, keep one shape check (an array of { code, name } with no GB). The behaviour is already covered by run-mode.test.js and the address-book tests.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. fixtures.test.js imports a checked-in JSON file and asserts its length (249), its first two entries and that JE is present. It tests no app behaviour and fails only on a legitimate recapture, which conflicts with Sam's no-coverage-padding and test-behaviour rules. The 'no GB' assertion has some value as a guard, since address-countries.js adds GB itself and a GB in the fixture would duplicate it. Keep that check plus a { code, name } shape check, and drop the count and order assertions.","also":[]},{"rank":32,"ranks":[32],"id":"feature-folders-f4","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"maxlength=5 on the region code suffix silently cuts off a code typed with its prefix","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/origin/template.njk","line":35,"what":"The suffix box now has maxlength 5. Server-side, suffixOf in origin/validate.js deliberately accepts a value typed with the country prefix ('FR-75'). With maxlength 5 the browser truncates that input before the server sees it, so the forgiving path can never receive a full prefixed code. GOV.UK guidance also advises against maxlength on text inputs because users are not told their input was cut.","scenario":"With France as the country, the trader pastes 'FR-12345' from the health certificate. The browser keeps 'FR-12'. suffixOf strips 'FR-', the page stores 'FR-12', and the review shows a wrong region code with no error.","severity":"low","category":"accessibility","fix":"Drop the maxlength attribute and rely on the existing server-side REGION_CODE_SUFFIX_MAX_LENGTH check, which reports the error. If the design insists on maxlength, remove the prefix-forgiving branch so behaviour is predictable, and update the fit test that asserts the attribute.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. The diff adds attributes: { maxlength: '5' } to origin/template.njk, while origin/validate.js:59-65 suffixOf still strips a typed 'FR-' prefix ('Also forgives a user who typed the prefix themselves'). The server-side REGION_CODE_SUFFIX_MAX_LENGTH = 5 check can never fire on a pasted 'FR-12345', because the browser has already cut it to 'FR-12'. That value passes as region code FR-12 with no error. The maxlength was requested in the origin-page backlog (row note at line 229), so this is a conflict in the spec as well as in the code.","also":[]},{"rank":33,"ranks":[33],"id":"plants-features-f2","area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Welsh view header shows English month names","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/high-risk-plants/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/controller.js","line":84,"what":"longDateText always formats with the 'en-GB' locale, but the new copy.cy viewHeader.dateCreated/dateSubmitted wrap it in Welsh. The Welsh page therefore mixes Welsh text with an English date.","scenario":"Draft viewed with the Welsh locale shows 'Dyddiad creu: 22 July 2026' instead of '22 Gorffennaf 2026'. A submitted view does the same with 'Dyddiad cyflwyno'.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Format with the request's locale ('cy' or 'cy-GB' when Welsh is active; Intl supports Welsh month names), or use a shared localised long-date helper, and add a cy assertion to the existing fake-timer test.","verifierNote":"Real. longDateText (controller.js:83-89) hardcodes 'en-GB', and the new copy.cy.js:18-19 wraps it in 'Dyddiad creu:' / 'Dyddiad cyflwyno:', so the Welsh page shows an English month name. confirmation/controller.js:25 and declaration/controller.js:29 already format with 'en-GB' in the same way, so the build copied an existing convention rather than adding a new kind of defect. A fix should be one shared localised date helper, not a change to this file alone.","also":[]},{"rank":34,"ranks":[34],"id":"feature-folders-f3","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Draft review page title contradicts inc-111's acceptance criterion","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/controller.js","line":148,"what":"headed is true for DRAFT, so pageTitle becomes the journey reference. Inc-111 says 'the journey review of a draft or an amendment keeps Review your notification - Import notification service - GOV.UK' (c-006). Inc-187 later changed the draft's h1 to the reference but says nothing about the <title>. The fit spec 'a draft's review is titled by its reference' pins the changed title, so a live acceptance criterion is now contradicted and that contradiction is locked in by a test.","scenario":"A trader opens a draft's review from the hub. The browser tab and the screen-reader page title read '<reference> - Import notification service - GOV.UK' (with 'Error: ' in front while incomplete), not 'Review your notification - …' as the inc-111 criterion requires.","severity":"low","category":"correctness","fix":"Get a ruling on which wins. If inc-111 stands, set pageTitle to copy.title for DRAFT while keeping the reference as the h1, and update the fit spec. If inc-187 supersedes it, update the inc-111 criterion and the openspec scenario so they agree.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. check-answers/controller.js sets headed = readOnly || status === DRAFT and title = headed ? journey.journeyId : reviewTitle(journey), and passes that as pageTitle. The overview-and-review backlog.json:996 (inc-111) still says the review of a draft 'keeps Review your notification - Import notification service - GOV.UK'. The draft-header row (backlog.json:1701-1711) changes only the h1 and says nothing about <title>. The criteria contradict each other, and the code follows one of them.","also":[]},{"rank":35,"ranks":[35],"id":"feature-folders-f5","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"species-errors imports a sibling feature's controller and repeats the obligation rule","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/view-model/species-errors.js","line":5,"what":"The new check-answers view-model module imports scopedFields from commodities/animal-identification/animal-identification.controller.js, which reaches into another feature's controller rather than its page.js descriptor. It also restates the obligation's multi-species identifier rule in the view model: lines.length <= 1 plus 'any unit carries any identifier' mirrors unitRecord.requires.floorAppliesToParent(moreThanOne(...)) in obligations/sections/commodities/identifiers.js. If the obligation changes, the error summary drifts from what actually blocks submission.","scenario":"The identifier floor is later changed, for example to count species rather than commodity lines, or to require an identifier on every unit. The review keeps naming 'Enter at least 1 animal identifier for <species>' by the old rule, or names nothing while Continue is refused. Separately, a refactor of the animal-identification controller breaks check-answers.","severity":"low","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Get the per-line verdict from the evaluator or scope (the same per-instance verdict the bridge's invariants compute) instead of re-deriving it. Move scopedFields to services/commodities, or to a non-controller module, so neither identifier-columns nor species-errors imports a controller.","verifierNote":"Confirmed. The new file check-answers/view-model/species-errors.js:5 imports scopedFields from commodities/animal-identification/animal-identification.controller.js, which reaches into another feature's controller. identifier-columns.js already did the same on main. It also re-derives the multi-species floor itself ('lines.length <= SINGLE_SPECIES' plus 'any unit carries any identifier'), and its own JSDoc says this is 'the same test the unit record's floor applies' (obligations/sections/commodities/identifiers.js:113 moreThanOne). The rule is now kept in two places that can drift apart. This is a design risk with no current wrong output, so low.","also":[]},{"rank":36,"ranks":[36],"id":"feature-folders-f6","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Hub wraps every task group in a named section, adding many region landmarks","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/hub/template.njk","line":32,"what":"The overview now wraps the totals block, each totals tile (aria-label) and every task-list group in a <section> with an accessible name. Each one becomes a region landmark, so the page goes from no regions to about nine nested ones. That clutters landmark navigation and is not a GOV.UK task list pattern.","scenario":"A screen-reader user opens the landmarks list on the Overview. They find 'Animals summary', 'Packages summary', the totals region, the task list region and one region per section group, all nested inside main, where previously the headings gave them the structure.","severity":"low","category":"accessibility","fix":"Keep the headings and remove the section wrappers (or use plain divs), leaving at most one labelled region if the design really needs one. Check with the accessibility lead before adding landmarks the GOV.UK task list pattern does not use.","verifierNote":"Partly mandated. backlog.json:118 (overview-and-review) requires the two tiles to be regions named 'Animals summary' and 'Packages summary', so those two sections are acceptance criteria. The extra wrappers are not required by any criterion: the totals section, the task-list section and one section per group, each with aria-labelledby. Each becomes a named region landmark. The landmark noise the reviewer describes does happen, but it is a minor accessibility concern. The fix should keep the two required tile regions.","also":[]},{"rank":37,"ranks":[37],"id":"feature-folders-f7","area":"Animals frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Explanatory and migration comments added to source and specs","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/transport/transit-countries/transit-countries.controller.js","line":38,"what":"The build added comments that explain behaviour or design history, against the house rule. Examples: '// Asked of rail and road consignments only…' (transit-countries.controller.js:38); '// The customs document code a live-animals import declaration quotes (CHED-A), fixed.' (view-header.js:16); the {# A submitted notification is a record… A draft ends with Continue… #} block at the end of check-answers/template.njk; the 'Design release 2.1 lays the review out…' and 'Design release 2.1 reads the countries…' comments in check-answers.fit.spec.js; '// The error summary lists errors by question…' (commodities/consignment-details/fields.js); and '// A radio cannot be un-chosen…' (import-reason/validate.js).","scenario":"The next change to these pages leaves the 'Design release 2.1' prose stating something the code no longer does. The prose is a migration narrative that belongs in docs/openspec, not the source.","severity":"low","category":"stray-comment","fix":"Delete them. Where the rationale matters, it already lives in the backlog and openspec, so rely on the names (transitApplies, CUSTOMS_DOCUMENT_CODE) and the tests.","verifierNote":"Confirmed for the cited example. The diff adds '// Asked of rail and road consignments only. Out of scope, the page has nothing to ask, so a load or a submit goes to the overview instead.' above transitApplies in transit-countries.controller.js. It also rewrites the existing countryItems explanatory comment instead of removing it. This breaks the house no-explanatory-comments rule. It is a hygiene issue only, so low.","also":[]},{"rank":38,"ranks":[38],"id":"plants-features-f3","area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"Confirmation now imports from the declaration feature folder","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/high-risk-plants/journeys/linear/features/confirmation/controller.js","line":11,"what":"The build added `import { declarationPage } from '../declaration/page.js'` so confirmation can redirect a non-submitted journey to the declaration page. That makes the confirmation feature depend directly on another feature folder, which breaks the rule that feature folders are independent.","scenario":"Renaming or moving the declaration feature, or changing its page descriptor, now breaks confirmation's import. The two folders can no longer be changed or deleted independently.","severity":"low","category":"cross-feature","fix":"Resolve the redirect through the flow layer, for example the review section's page order in flow/flow.js or a slug constant in shared kit like CYA_SLUG/CANCEL_AMEND_SLUG, rather than importing another feature's page.js.","verifierNote":"The diff adds `import { declarationPage } from '../declaration/page.js'` at confirmation/controller.js:11, replacing kit.CYA_SLUG. That is a new cross-feature dependency against the house rule. Cross-feature imports already exist (check-answers imports origin/controller.js and review/lateness.js, and dashboard imports commodity-type/page.js), so the rule is already bent in places, but this one is new. The fix suggested (a slug constant in kit, like CYA_SLUG and CANCEL_AMEND_SLUG) fits. Low.","also":[]},{"rank":39,"ranks":[39],"id":"plants-features-f4","area":"Plants frontend feature folders (sample)","priority":3,"title":"New ruling-ID and explanatory comments in check-answers controller","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/high-risk-plants/journeys/linear/features/check-answers/controller.js","line":150,"what":"The build added comments that cite ruling IDs or explain the code: '// c-201: an amendment opens with no errors; c-136: Continue refused shows them.', '// A UTC instant shown as the Europe/London civil date.', and a rewritten '// Ruled c-030: ...'. commodity-type/controller.js:84 gains a comment ('A blank type only reaches here on Save and return to overview with no type ever saved') that states something the code never checks. The house rules say no explanatory or migration comments; the reasoning belongs in docs and git history.","scenario":"Once ruling IDs c-201/c-136 are renumbered or superseded, the comment points readers at the wrong decision. The commodity-type comment will go stale silently if the radios ever become clearable.","severity":"low","category":"stray-comment","fix":"Remove these comments. Name the branch instead (holdsSilently already says what it does), and keep the c-xxx references in the openspec/plan, not in source.","verifierNote":"The diff confirms these lines are new: '// c-201: an amendment opens with no errors; c-136: ...' (controller.js:150), '// A UTC instant shown as the Europe/London civil date.' (line 82), the rewritten '// Ruled c-030' comment (lines 180-181, which existed before but was edited), and the commodity-type comment (lines 84-85). These are the explanatory and ruling-reference comments the house rules ban. One correction: the commodity-type comment is accurate today, because validate refuses a blank type unless allowMissing (a hub exit) is set. It is a rule breach, not a false statement. Low.","also":[]},{"rank":40,"ranks":[40],"id":"found-in-capture-f1","area":"Found while capturing screenshots (pre-existing on main)","priority":2,"title":"A document reference with a hyphen gives the trader an error page instead of a validation message","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/app/sets/live-animals/journeys/linear/features/documents","line":1,"what":"The animals backend accepts only letters and digits in a document reference (@Pattern ^[a-zA-Z0-9]*$ on documentReference), but the animals frontend has no matching rule. The backend's 400 surfaces as a 500 from the frontend's accompanying-documents POST, and the page says 'The file could not be uploaded. Try again.' This is on main too, so the build did not introduce it; the engineering review's tests-repo verifier flagged the same gap.","scenario":"A trader uploads an ITAHC with reference VHC-2026-0001 (or UK-123). The POST to /live-animals/notifications/{id}/accompanying-documents returns 500 and the trader sees a generic upload failure with no hint that the hyphen is the problem. Reproduced on both main (published images) and the combined branch on 12 October.","severity":"medium","category":"correctness","fix":"Validate the reference in the frontend with the backend's rule and a specific error message (or widen the backend rule if hyphens are legitimate in real certificate numbers — ask the business), and map a backend 400 to a page error rather than a 500. Add a fit test for the hyphenated case.","verifierNote":"Reproduced directly by the screenshot harness on both branches (screens/before/hyphen-check.json); not run through the adversarial verifier.","also":[]}],"killed":[{"id":"tests-repo-f2","area":"Tests repo","title":"INS dashboard test locks in the raw status enum shown to users","severity":"medium","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"tests/ins/e2e/features/notification-dashboard-navigation.spec.ts","line":36,"refutation":"The raw enum in the INS table cell (features/dashboard/controller.js:54) is on main, and the build did not change it. The ins-frontend fit spec on main already asserts raw 'SUBMITTED' cells. Sam's ruling ruling-sam-2026-10-09-pm.md section (j), point 52, blocks the INS dashboard rows until its requirements firm up and are re-distilled, so the raw-enum table is the accepted interim display. The new toContainText('SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED') proves the aggregated read model caught up to the new status, which is a real behaviour worth checking. A future change to a readable label would update this assertion alongside the fit spec, which is normal test maintenance and not a defect. The finding's wider point (the INS dashboard shows raw enums) is a known, ruled gap and not something this diff introduced."},{"id":"tests-repo-f4","area":"Tests repo","title":"notificationView.heading now matches any review or view page","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-ins-tests","file":"page-objects/animals/notification-view-page.ts","line":13,"refutation":"The widened heading follows the design, which gives a draft's review and a submitted view the same H1 (the reference), so a heading-only locator cannot tell them apart. The cited scenario is refuted. In addresses-submit-freeze.spec.ts, the heading check at :65 after amendNotification is followed at once by statusTag toHaveText('Amend') at :66, and the same holds at :126/:128. A misroute to the read-only submitted view would fail there ('Submitted action required'). change-from-cya pairs the heading check with toHaveURL, as the reviewer says. No spec was shown to rely on heading alone to tell states apart, so this is a theoretical proxy risk, not a demonstrated weakness."},{"id":"animals-fe-shared-f2","area":"Animals frontend shared code","title":"The error handler logs a plain string, so the err serialiser never runs","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-animals-frontend","file":"src/server/common/helpers/errors.js","line":50,"refutation":"The main claim is not a regression from this diff. On main the line was already request.logger.error(response?.stack), which also logs a plain string with no { err } object, so the ECS error.* fields were already missing before the build. The change only adds the cause stacks to a message that was already a string, which is an improvement on main. The infinite-loop part needs a cause chain that loops back on itself. Neither undici's 'fetch failed' wrapping nor Boom produces one, and the reviewer gives no real source. Switching to { err: response } is a fair hardening suggestion, but no failure scenario holds for code this diff introduced."},{"id":"plants-shared-f1","area":"Plants frontend shared code","title":"stackWithCauses loops forever on a cyclic error cause","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/common/helpers/errors.js","line":18,"refutation":"The loop in src/server/common/helpers/errors.js:18-27 does have no cycle guard, but the review gives no way to make it spin. I grepped every place plants src sets a cause: get-oidc-config-with-retry.js:20, services/countries/index.js:27 and services/ports/index.js:26. Each wraps a fresh `new Error(..., { cause: err })` one level deep, and nothing assigns `.cause` back up the chain. undici's 'fetch failed' errors also have acyclic causes. The 'retry wrapper' in the scenario does not exist in this codebase. It is a cheap hardening (keep a Set of seen causes), and backend-fetch.js:30-35 walks causes the same unguarded way, but it is not a defect the diff makes reachable."},{"id":"plants-shared-f2","area":"Plants frontend shared code","title":"Amend 'Save and return' keeps required checks in plants but skips them in animals","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-plants-frontend","file":"src/server/app/shared/kit.js","line":126,"refutation":"The facts are right. Plants kit.js has only isHubExit (line 126). arrival-details/controller.js:192 and the other plants pages pass `allowMissing: kit.isHubExit(request)`, and their templates use amendFormActions. So on plants, an amend 'Save and return' (?change=1) with a required answer blank is refused. But that is the plants increment working as scoped, not a shared-code defect. journey-foundation inc-003 (plans/inc-003.md) scopes soft save to 'Save and return to overview' only, and its AC5 for plants proves exactly that. Animals' skipsRequiredChecks came from a separate per-page increment (fa115bd6, inc-022, commodities-and-reason theme) and is used only on import-reason. Every other animals page also refuses a blank on amend 'Save and return', so the gap is within animals as well as between the two frontends, not a plants regression. It is worth one line to Sam as an open product question (the prototype treats the review as a soft-save action), but it is not a defect in the changed shared code."},{"id":"ins-frontend-f3","area":"INS frontend","title":"stackWithCauses loops forever on a cause chain that cycles","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/common/helpers/errors.js","line":24,"refutation":"The missing cycle guard is real in principle, but I found nothing that can produce a cyclic cause. The only cause producers are undici fetch errors (a linear chain), get-oidc-config-with-retry.js and countries/index.js, which puts its cause in data so the loop never sees it. No retry or wrapping code here assigns err.cause back up the chain. It is a hardening suggestion, not a demonstrable defect. Adding a Set guard is cheap if someone is already changing the function."},{"id":"ins-frontend-f4","area":"INS frontend","title":"New status shown to users as the raw enum 'SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED'","severity":"low","repo":"trade-imports-ins-frontend","file":"src/server/app/features/dashboard/view-model/list.js","line":112,"refutation":"list.js:112 is not touched by the diff, and every status already shows as its raw code. inc-113's plan decides this deliberately (decision 12: 'The status cell keeps printing the raw code, as it does for every status today. The dashboard template and copy belong to another theme'), and its out-of-scope list repeats it. The fit test asserting 'SUBMITTED_ACTION_REQUIRED' matches the existing DRAFT/AMEND/SUBMITTED assertions. This is a real, existing GDS gap for the theme that owns dashboard copy, not a defect this build introduced."}]};
